{%- assign tool_name = include.tool | default: page.tool | default: "tool" -%}
{%- assign exec_name = include.exec | default: "tool" -%}
{%- assign env_name = include.env | default: "env_tool" -%}
{%- assign env_base = "/reference/workbook" -%}
{%- assign env_root = include.env_root | default: "<subject_area>" -%}
{%- assign user_env_root = include.user_env_root | default: "/90daydata/shared/$USER/envs" -%}
{%- assign container_base = "/reference/containers/" -%}
{%- assign controls_id = include.controls | default: tool_name | slugify | append: "-find-tool-acc" -%}

<div class="usa-accordion" data-allow-multiple>
{% include accordion title="Tools can be found in modules, containers or virtual environments" class="note" icon=true controls=controls_id %}
<div id="{{ controls_id }}" class="accordion_content" style="font-size: 80%;" markdown='1' hidden>

Several setup routes are common on SCINet HPC:

- Use the ready-made environment first if it is available under `{{ env_base }}/{{ env_root }}/env/venv/` or `{{ env_base }}/{{ env_root }}/env/conda/`.
- Some tools may already be on your PATH; check with `which <tool>` and use them directly if available.
```bash
which {{ exec_name }}
```
- Otherwise, check whether SCINet provides the `{{ tool_name }}` as a module: 
```bash
module spider {{ exec_name }}
module avail {{ exec_name }}
module load {{ exec_name }}     # load the module, if it is available
```
- If `{{ tool_name }}` is not available as a module, check whether it is available as a container image:
```bash
ls {{ container_base }}     # list available containers
module load apptainer
apptainer exec {{ container_base }}<tool>/<version>/<image>.simg {{ exec_name }} --help
```
- If `{{ tool_name }}` is not available yet, install it only inside a user-managed environment such as `{{ user_env_root }}/{{ env_name }}` or project space (`/project/<project_name>/software/envs/{{ env_name }}`), not in `/home`.
</div>
</div>
